  "summary_en": "Philosophical article using social ontology (collective end theory, normative-teleological theory of institutions) to assess whether robots can engage in joint action or occupy institutional roles with humans. It argues robots are not moral agents, cannot bear moral responsibility, and should remain tightly human-controlled instruments.",
  "key_provisions_or_claims": [
   "Robots are not moral or rational agents and cannot be ascribed moral responsibility.",
   "Robots cannot properly occupy institutional roles or hold institutional authority, rights and duties.",
   "Robots can only perform simple joint-like tasks, not morally significant joint actions.",
   "Robots should be technological instruments under tight human control serving institutions' collective goods.",
   "Analysis is restricted to existing robot technology, not speculative future robots."
  ],
